"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][36]={"university_physics_en:M36-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M36-L1 – Standard Model, Gauge Groups and Electric Charge<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30: quantum field theory; M34:\nquantum states.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute the electric charges of\na left-chiral lepton doublet using a specified hypercharge\nconvention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>The Standard Model arranges matter fields and their interactions in a\ngauge structure. The strong interaction uses SU(3), while electroweak\ntheory uses SU(2) × U(1). Electroweak symmetry breaking produces massive\nW and Z bosons and a massless photon; Higgs couplings are relevant for\nfermion masses. A gauge group is not a table of measured particle\nmasses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Adopt the convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=T_3+Y/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor electric charge in units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a left-chiral lepton doublet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\nu_L,e_L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the upper and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the lower entry. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(\\nu_L)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(e_L)=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSome references define hypercharge without a factor of two; the physical\ncharge does not change.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=T_3+\\frac{Y}{2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For the doublet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=(\\nu_L,e_L)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe charges are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1/2-1/2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/2-1/2=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe original diagram separates the two components and their\ncontributions. This is a diagram of quantum numbers rather than a\nmeasured energy spectrum.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_276_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two left-chiral lepton-doublet components combine weak isospin and hypercharge contributions to give zero and minus one.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two left-chiral lepton-doublet components\ncombine weak isospin and hypercharge contributions to give zero and\nminus one.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic lepton-doublet charge accounting with a fixed\nhypercharge convention.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the upper component\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_3=+1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nof a left-handed quark doublet with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor its lower component.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere not be combined with the different convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=T_3+Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=+2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=-1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">That mixes normalizations; all quantum numbers must use the same\nconvention.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation concerns weak-isospin states in a fixed convention.\nIt does not determine flavour mixing or numerical couplings.\nRight-handed lepton singlets have different SU(2) quantum numbers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Unstable particles offer an experimental route into interaction\nprocesses. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/reviews/standard_model_and_related.html\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026: Standard Model &amp; Related Topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Review index for gauge interactions, quarks and neutrinos; course equations are derived independently; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L1 – Standard Model, Gauge Groups and Electric Charge · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M36-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M36-L2 – Unstable Particles, Lifetime and Decay Law<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: statistical distributions; M30:\ninteractions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish mean lifetime from\nhalf-life and compute an idealized survival probability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Unstable particles decay randomly; an individual lifetime cannot be\npredicted. An ensemble with a constant decay hazard follows an\nexponential law. Mean lifetime and half-life are distinct descriptors.\nAt colliders, production, flight time and detection also depend on\nparticle motion and detector selection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a constant decay hazard\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1/\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin proper time,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dN/dt=-\\lambda N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegration gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)=N_0e^{-t/\\tau}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand survival probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{surv}}(t)=e^{-t/\\tau}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe half-life satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{surv}}(t_{1/2})=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{1/2}=\\tau\\ln2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRelativistic particles require proper time to be distinguished from\nlaboratory time through time dilation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{surv}}(t)=e^{-t/\\tau},\\qquad t_{1/2}=\\tau\\ln 2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>In a constructed example take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter a proper time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe average surviving fraction of a large ensemble is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>36.8<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}\\approx36.8\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe half-life is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.386<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.386\\,\\mu\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram describes a normalized ensemble, not a measurement of a\nparticular particle.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_306_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponentially falling survival curve with mean lifetime and half-life marked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponentially falling survival curve with\nmean lifetime and half-life marked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ideal exponential survival curve for an unstable\nensemble.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{surv}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the half-life for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=4\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does this proper-time expression directly use laboratory time for a\nrelativistic beam?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1353<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2}\\approx0.1353<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.773<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\ln2\\approx2.773\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; relativistic time dilation must be incorporated.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The exponential model assumes a stationary hazard without important\nselection effects. The number surviving is random;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives an expectation, not a deterministic particle list.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Experiments reconstruct decay products from tracks and energy\ndeposits. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/reviews/standard_model_and_related.html\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026: Standard Model &amp; Related Topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Review index for gauge interactions, quarks and neutrinos; course equations are derived independently; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L2 – Unstable Particles, Lifetime and Decay Law · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Reference portal for particle physics and statistics; no tables or illustrations reused; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L2 – Unstable Particles, Lifetime and Decay Law · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M36-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M36-L3 – Scattering Kinematics, Pseudorapidity and Jet Separation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30: scattering processes; M31:\nnumerical reconstruction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute angular separation in\npseudorapidity–azimuth space for two reconstructed directions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Collider detectors measure tracks and energy deposits from which\nobjects such as jets are reconstructed. A direction relative to the beam\naxis is often expressed by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=-\\ln\\tan(\\theta/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAzimuth is periodic. The quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a distance in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\eta,\\phi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncoordinate space, not a literal three-dimensional opening angle or a\ncomplete jet algorithm.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Wrap the azimuth difference onto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-\\pi,\\pi]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\eta=\\eta_1-\\eta_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R=\\sqrt{(\\Delta\\eta)^2+(\\Delta\\phi)^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nGeometrical proximity alone does not determine\nanti-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nclustering: the algorithm additionally uses transverse momenta and a\nspecific iterative distance measure.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R=\\sqrt{(\\eta_1-\\eta_2)^2+(\\Delta\\phi)^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Take two constructed directions with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_1=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_2=1.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_1=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_2=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nradians. The differences have magnitudes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Delta\\eta|=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Delta\\phi|=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram uses an illustrative radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto depict proximity.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_045_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two points in pseudorapidity–azimuth space separated by 0.4 and 0.3, shown with radius 0.6.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two points in pseudorapidity–azimuth\nspace separated by 0.4 and 0.3, shown with radius 0.6.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustrative distance in jet coordinates; not a clustering\nsimulation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\eta=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\phi=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the difference between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>179<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">179^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mn>179<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-179^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe wrapped?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R&lt;R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na complete\nanti-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalgorithm?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The short azimuth difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>358<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">358^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; transverse momenta and clustering steps are also required.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Pseudorapidity approximates rapidity when particle mass is small\ncompared with momentum. Detector acceptance, resolution, calibration and\nbackground affect reconstructed jets. The drawing illustrates a\ngeometric criterion only.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Neutrinos usually leave no direct track and require other\nobservables. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.cern/science/physics/12-steps-idea-discovery/\">CERN – 12 Steps from Idea to Discovery<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Track and jet reconstruction, simulated signals and statistical checks; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L3 – Scattering Kinematics, Pseudorapidity and Jet Separation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/reviews/experimental_methods_and_colliders.html\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026: Experimental Methods &amp; Colliders<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Review index for detectors, accelerators and collider experiments; topical coverage only; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L3 – Scattering Kinematics, Pseudorapidity and Jet Separation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M36-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M36-L4 – Neutrino Oscillations and Mass Mixing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M34: quantum superposition; M30:\nStandard Model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate an idealized\ntwo-flavour transition probability using consistent units.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Neutrino flavour states are superpositions of mass eigenstates. Their\ndifferent propagation phases can change the measured flavour over a\nbaseline. This is interference and evidence for nondegenerate neutrino\nmasses. Real experiments generally require three flavours, matter\neffects and detector modelling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In vacuum and the two-flavour approximation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\nu_\\alpha\\to\\nu_\\beta)=\\sin^2(2\\theta)\\sin^2(\\Delta m^2L/4E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin natural units. When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis entered in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{eV}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{GeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe phase becomes numerically\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.267<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.267\\,\\Delta m^2L/E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe unit prescription is part of the formula;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.267<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.267<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a fundamental dimensionless constant.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.267<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\alpha\\to\\beta}=\\sin^2(2\\theta)\\,\\sin^2\\!\\left(1.267\\,\\frac{\\Delta m^2[\\mathrm{eV}^2]L[\\mathrm{km}]}{E[\\mathrm{GeV}]}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For maximal mixing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m^2=2.5\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{eV}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=500\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe phase is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.58375<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.58375<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.9998<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\approx0.9998<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure plots the ideal oscillation pattern against\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L/E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot a detector cross-section.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_187_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Squared-sine neutrino transition probability versus distance at maximal mixing.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Squared-sine neutrino transition\nprobability versus distance at maximal mixing.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Two-flavour vacuum oscillation with illustrative\nparameters.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the oscillation phase change when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What length unit is required in the numerical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.267<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.267<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nformula?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Kilometres.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The two-flavour vacuum model neglects three-flavour interference, the\nCP phase, matter effects and finite energy resolution. The parameters\nhere serve as a calculation example, not a current global fit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Heavy-ion collisions provide a different window into many-body\ndynamics. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/reviews/standard_model_and_related.html\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026: Standard Model &amp; Related Topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Review index for gauge interactions, quarks and neutrinos; course equations are derived independently; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L4 – Neutrino Oscillations and Mass Mixing · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M36-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M36-L5 – Heavy-Ion Collisions, Quark–Gluon Plasma and Flow<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M27: many-body theory; M30: QCD.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain elliptic flow as a\nFourier moment and evaluate a normalized angular model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Ultrarelativistic nucleus–nucleus collisions can briefly produce\nstrongly interacting matter at extreme energy density. Experiments such\nas ALICE study collective particle distributions and jet modifications\nto constrain quark–gluon plasma properties. An anisotropic distribution\nis a class of observable, not by itself a complete phase diagnosis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For azimuth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to event plane\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Psi_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndefine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=\\langle\\cos 2(\\varphi-\\Psi_2)\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA simplified normalized single-harmonic model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\varphi)=(1+2v_2\\cos2(\\varphi-\\Psi_2))/(2\\pi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts integral from zero to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals one. For this single-harmonic truncation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|v_2|\\le1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis needed for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nmeasured distributions use more harmonics and corrections.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=\\left\\langle\\cos\\bigl(2(\\varphi-\\Psi_2)\\bigr)\\right\\rangle.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Psi_2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe unnormalized angular density is proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.8<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na ratio of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe polar curve is illustrative and is not derived from measured\ndata.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_130_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Closed polar distribution with a mild elliptic anisotropy and two principal axes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Closed polar distribution with a mild\nelliptic anisotropy and two principal axes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Constructed elliptic-flow distribution with v2 equal to\n0.10.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a perfectly uniform azimuthal distribution?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+2v_2)/(1-2v_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does elliptic flow alone not uniquely establish quark–gluon\nplasma formation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.33<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.4/0.6=7/3\\approx2.33<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Other effects, system size and modelling assumptions must be\ncontrolled.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The illustration ignores event-plane fluctuations, nonflow\ncorrelations, collision geometry and higher harmonics. Hydrodynamic\nclaims require multiple observables and model comparisons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The next topic is controlled formation of particle beams before\ncollision. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://alice.cern/alice-physics/\">CERN ALICE – ALICE Physics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Heavy-ion collisions and strongly interacting matter; not a derivation of the flow formula; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L5 – Heavy-Ion Collisions, Quark–Gluon Plasma and Flow · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.cern/science/physics/heavy-ions-and-quark-gluon-plasma/\">CERN – Heavy Ions and Quark-Gluon Plasma<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Heavy-ion experiments and jet quenching as experimental context; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L5 – Heavy-Ion Collisions, Quark–Gluon Plasma and Flow · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M36-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M36-L6 – Transverse Beam Optics and RMS Emittance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31: linear systems and numerics;\nM32: accelerator instrumentation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate geometric RMS\nemittance from centered phase-space moments and explain conditional\ninvariance under linear transport.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A charged particle beam is described transversely by position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand small slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo>≃<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\prime}\\simeq p_x/p_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the paraxial thin-focusing-lens model the slope changes as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>′<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>′<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\prime}_{\\mathrm{out}}=x^{\\prime}_{\\mathrm{in}}-x/f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unchanged at the lens. A beam is a phase-space distribution rather\nthan a single trajectory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For centered variables\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=x-\\langle x\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^{\\prime}=x^{\\prime}-\\langle x^{\\prime}\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe covariance matrix is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>′<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma=\\begin{pmatrix}\\langle X^2\\rangle&amp;\\langle XX^{\\prime}\\rangle\\\\\\langle XX^{\\prime}\\rangle&amp;\\langle X^{\\prime2}\\rangle\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nGeometric RMS emittance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{rms}}=\\sqrt{\\det\\Sigma}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(e.g. mm mrad). Under linear transport\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{out}}=R\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{in}}R^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}=|\\det R|\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA thin lens has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\-1/f&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand determinant one.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>′<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{rms}}=\\sqrt{\\langle X^2\\rangle\\langle X^{\\prime2}\\rangle-\\langle XX^{\\prime}\\rangle^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x=2\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{x^{\\prime}}=1\\,\\mathrm{mrad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand correlation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle XX^{\\prime}\\rangle=\\rho\\sigma_x\\sigma_{x^{\\prime}}=1.2\\,\\mathrm{mm\\,mrad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.6<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{rms}}=2\\sqrt{1-0.6^2}=1.6\\,\\mathrm{mm\\,mrad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe original image shows a schematic phase-space covariance ellipse; the\nenclosed area depends on the selected contour.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_300_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Tilted ellipse in transverse phase space, centered at zero with RMS axes marked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Tilted ellipse in transverse phase space,\ncentered at zero with RMS axes marked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Covariance ellipse for a constructed transverse beam\ndistribution.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find RMS emittance at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same spreads.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det R=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange the magnitude of RMS emittance for a linear map?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is geometric emittance not automatically invariant during\nacceleration?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mm\\,mrad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its magnitude is unchanged because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\det R|=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo>≃<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\prime}\\simeq p_x/p_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscales with longitudinal momentum; normalized emittance needs separate\nassumptions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This result describes a decoupled linear 2D phase plane in fixed\ncoordinates without significant nonlinear forces, space charge or\nscattering. Acceleration, coupling and nonlinear dynamics prevent a\nblanket invariance claim.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Detectors subsequently reconstruct curved trajectories of collision\nproducts. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://videos.cern.ch/record/3025960\">CERN CDS – Transverse Beam Dynamics III<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Metadata entry on transverse beam dynamics; not treated as proof of an emittance formula; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L6 – Transverse Beam Optics and RMS Emittance · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/reviews/experimental_methods_and_colliders.html\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026: Experimental Methods &amp; Colliders<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Review index for detectors, accelerators and collider experiments; topical coverage only; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L6 – Transverse Beam Optics and RMS Emittance · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M36-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M36-L7 – Tracking Detectors, Magnetic Curvature and Momentum<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10: Lorentz force; M36-L6: particle\nbeams.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute transverse momentum from\nan ideal circular-track radius in a uniform magnetic field.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Trackers register hit positions from which charged-particle\ntrajectories are reconstructed inside a magnetic field. Bending\norientation depends on charge sign, while radius constrains momentum at\nknown field strength. Calorimeters measure deposited energy and muon\nchambers detect penetrating charged particles. These subsystems are\ncomplementary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntransverse momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand uniform magnetic field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|v_TB=p_Tv_T/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T=|q|BR<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn practical units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>0.2998<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T[\\mathrm{GeV}/c]\\simeq0.2998\\,|q/e|B[\\mathrm T]R[\\mathrm m]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rounded\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomes from unit conversion, not an empirical fit.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>0.2998<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T[\\mathrm{GeV}/c]\\simeq0.2998\\,|q/e|\\,B[\\mathrm T]R[\\mathrm m].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a singly charged particle at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=2\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T\\approx0.600\\,\\mathrm{GeV}/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA second track of twice the radius at the same field and charge would\nhave twice the transverse momentum. The figure is an original schematic\nof detector layers and two bent tracks.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_299_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic tracking-detector rings with two charged curved tracks and magnetic-field direction.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic tracking-detector rings with\ntwo charged curved tracks and magnetic-field direction.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Simplified magnetic-track detection in a collider\ndetector.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=2\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|=e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the radius for a singly charged track with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T=1.2\\,\\mathrm{GeV}/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=2\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a neutral particle not leave a magnetic-curvature\nsignature?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T\\approx0.300\\,\\mathrm{GeV}/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\approx2.00\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With no charge there is no magnetic Lorentz force; neutral particles\nrequire other detection methods.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>A circular path assumes constant field, negligible losses and a known\nprojection. Real track fits require field maps, multiple-scattering and\nmaterial corrections, alignment and uncertainty estimates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The final lesson addresses statistical signal–background\ndiscrimination. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.cern/science/experiments/how-detector-works/\">CERN – How a Detector Works<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Tracking, calorimeters and particle identification; model example is independent; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L7 – Tracking Detectors, Magnetic Curvature and Momentum · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/reviews/experimental_methods_and_colliders.html\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026: Experimental Methods &amp; Colliders<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Review index for detectors, accelerators and collider experiments; topical coverage only; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L7 – Tracking Detectors, Magnetic Curvature and Momentum · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M36-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M36-L8 – Counting Experiments, Likelihood and Statistical Evidence<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M36 – Master: Particle Physics, Collision Experiments and Accelerators<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: statistics; M31: model\nvalidation; M32: counting measurements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply the Asimov formula for a\nsimple counting channel with known background and state its\nlimitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Particle experiments distinguish candidate signals from known and\nunknown backgrounds. A simple counting channel is often described by a\nPoisson likelihood. Evidential strength depends not only on count excess\nbut also background uncertainty and the statistical test. An observed\nexcess is not automatically a discovery.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For assumed signal expectation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand exactly known background\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na simple Asimov counting model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_A=\\sqrt{2[(s+b)\\ln(1+s/b)-s]}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>≪<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\ll b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>b<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/\\sqrt b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is the expected asymptotic significance in the model, neither an\nobserved p-value nor a universal formula under uncertain background.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>s<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_A=\\sqrt{2\\left[(s+b)\\ln\\!\\left(1+\\frac{s}{b}\\right)-s\\right]}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a hypothetical expected signal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.405<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_A\\approx2.405<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>b<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/\\sqrt b=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis slightly larger. The graphic compares the two model values, not an\nactual experimental significance.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_055_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two comparison bars for expected Asimov significance and its small-signal approximation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two comparison bars for expected Asimov\nsignificance and its small-signal approximation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Constructed likelihood calculation, not an experimental\nresult.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the approximate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>b<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/\\sqrt b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=10,b=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does that approximation change if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nquadruples at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the Asimov formula not be used without modification when\nbackground has uncertainty?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The likelihood must incorporate background nuisance parameters and\nconstraints.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Assumptions are a single Poisson count, known background and an\nasymptotic interpretation. Look-elsewhere effects, systematic\nuncertainties, selection bias and multiple testing require separate\ntreatment. Neither the example nor its image reports experimental\nsignificance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M36 thus links Standard Model concepts with collisions, detection and\ncareful data interpretation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2026/\">Particle Data Group – Review of Particle Physics 2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Reference portal for particle physics and statistics; no tables or illustrations reused; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L8 – Counting Experiments, Likelihood and Statistical Evidence · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://home.cern/science/physics/12-steps-idea-discovery/\">CERN – 12 Steps from Idea to Discovery<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Track and jet reconstruction, simulated signals and statistical checks; Subject relevance to lesson: M36-L8 – Counting Experiments, Likelihood and Statistical Evidence · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
